    21	## Definitions and conventions
    22	
    23	At a fixed angular frequency, let the two transverse components of a stationary source have cross-spectral density
    24	
    25	\[
    26	W_{ij}(r_1,r_2)=\mathbb E[E_i(r_1)E_j(r_2)^*].
    27	\]
    28	
    29	This convention is the complex conjugate/transposed convention of some optics references; observables are unchanged. A physically realizable source has a positive-semidefinite matrix kernel. A polarization-independent deterministic scalar kernel produces
    30	
    31	\[
    32	J_{ij}(x)=\iint K(x,r_1)W_{ij}(r_1,r_2)K(x,r_2)^*\,dr_1dr_2.
    33	\]
    34	
    35	Define intensity \(I=\operatorname{tr}J\), and, **only where \(I>0\)**,
    36	
    37	\[
    38	P(J)=\sqrt{1-4\det J/(\operatorname{tr}J)^2}
    39	     =|\boldsymbol s|/s_0,\qquad
    40	J=(s_0I_2+\boldsymbol s\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma)/2.
    41	\]
    42	
    43	`P` is the **local, ensemble-averaged, two-component spectral** degree of polarization. It is not the polarization of an instantaneous deterministic field, a spatially integrated Stokes vector, or a three-dimensional Maxwell polarization measure. A zero-intensity observation has no defined normalized spectrum or degree of polarization and is excluded, not assigned zero.
    44	
    45	Normalized-spectrum invariance over an observation set means \(I(x,\omega)=A(x)B(\omega)\), with nonnegative factors and a finite, nonzero integral of \(B\). This is equivalent to \(I(x,\omega)/\int I(x,\nu)d\nu\) being independent of every illuminated \(x\). It does not automatically mean equality to each point's source-plane spectrum.
    46	
    47	## C1. Source-domain spectral criterion without a Schell assumption
    48	
    49	**Evidence: Certification. Novelty: no priority claim; a Fourier reformulation/generalization of the standard far-field scaling argument, not an improvement over an already known physical law.**
    50	
    51	Work in two transverse dimensions, in the ideal scalar/paraxial Fraunhofer model, at a common propagation distance. Suppress the frequency-independent factor \((2\pi z)^{-2}\). The transverse angular coordinate \(s=x/z\) ranges over **all of \(\mathbb R^2\)** in this mathematical model. Put \(k=\omega/c>0\),
    52	
    53	\[
    54	G(v;k)=\int_{\mathbb R^2}\operatorname{tr}W(u+v/2,u-v/2;k)\,du,
    55	\quad
    56	\widehat G(q;k)=\int G(v;k)e^{-iq\cdot v}\,dv.
    57	\]
    58	
    59	Assume these functions and their Fourier transforms are integrable so that Fourier inversion and the displayed exchanges of integrals apply. Assume a nonzero source and nonzero finite total power. Then
    60	
    61	\[
    62	I(s;k)=k^2\widehat G(ks;k).
    63	\]
    64	
    65	For a specified nonnegative spectral factor \(B(k)\), the following are equivalent:
    66	
    67	1. \(I(s;k)=A(s)B(k)\) for all \(s,k\) (up to the usual almost-everywhere interpretation).
    68	2. There is one function \(g\), independent of \(k\), such that
    69	   \[
    70	   G(v;k)=B(k)g(kv).
    71	   \]
    72	
    73	**Proof.** Substitute \(r_1=u+v/2,r_2=u-v/2\) in the Fraunhofer double integral; its Jacobian is one and the phase is \(-ik s\cdot v\), proving the intensity formula. If (2) holds, the change of variable \(t=kv\) gives \(\widehat G(q;k)=B(k)k^{-2}\widehat g(q/k)\), hence (1) with \(A=\widehat g\). Conversely, (1) gives \(\widehat G(q;k)=B(k)k^{-2}A(q/k)\). Fourier inversion and \(q=kt\) give (2), with \(g(v)=(2\pi)^{-2}\int A(t)e^{it\cdot v}dt\). This proves both directions. Since \(G(0;k)\) is the source's spatially integrated spectral power, \(B(k)\) is proportional to that power when \(g(0)>0\). It need not equal the local source spectrum. ∎
    74	
    75	**Limits.** Only the midpoint-integrated trace is observable in far-zone intensity; no necessity for the *entire* source coherence function follows. In a nonparaxial physical hemisphere, only \(|q|\leq k\) is sampled; an inverse-Fourier necessity on all \(q\) is then invalid without an additional support/analyticity hypothesis. Obliquity factors and vector projections must also be included in a full-Maxwell theorem. No such theorem is asserted here.
    76	
    77	## C2. Complete finite-mode classification for every deterministic scalar probe
    78	
    79	**Evidence: Certification (complete proof below). Novelty: NOVELTY-UNCLEAR; closely related to standard constant-output-purity quantum-channel geometry.**
    80	
    81	Let \(\phi_1,\ldots,\phi_n\) be linearly independent square-integrable scalar source modes and
    82	
    83	\[
    84	E_i(r)=\sum_a c_{ai}\phi_a(r),\qquad
    85	\Gamma_{ai,bj}=\mathbb E[c_{ai}c_{bj}^*],\qquad \Gamma\succeq0.
    86	\]
    87	
    88	Let \(\Gamma_{ab}\) be its \(2\times2\) polarization blocks. A deterministic scalar probe with amplitudes \(f_a=\int K(r)\phi_a(r)dr\) gives
    89	
    90	\[
    91	J(f)=\Phi(ff^\dagger),\qquad
    92	\Phi(X)=\sum_{a,b}X_{ab}\Gamma_{ab}.
    93	\]
    94	
    95	The block matrix \(\Gamma\) is the Choi matrix of \(\Phi\); thus \(\Phi\) is completely positive. This also follows directly by decomposing \(\Gamma\) into nonnegative outer products: each term gives \(A X A^\dagger\). Let \(T=\Phi^*(I_2)\succeq0\), so \(\operatorname{tr}\Phi(X)=\operatorname{tr}(TX)\). The trace support dimension is \(r=\operatorname{rank}T\), **not** the rank of \(\Gamma\). The zero source \(r=0\) is excluded.
    96	
    97	For a vector in \(\ker T\), the positive matrix \(\Phi(ff^\dagger)\) has zero trace and is zero. In a Kraus representation this implies every \(A f=0\); consequently all mixed terms involving that kernel vanish too. Restrict to the support and define the trace-preserving completely positive map
    98	
    99	\[
   100	\Psi(X)=\Phi(T^{-1/2}XT^{-1/2}).
   101	\]
   102	
   103	For \(0\leq p\leq1\), \(P[J(f)]=p\) for **every** \(f\) with \(f^\dagger T f>0\) if and only if the whitened map is one of:
   104	
   105	**A. Replacement / factorized family, any \(r\):**
   106	\[
   107	\Psi(X)=\operatorname{tr}(X)C,\quad C\succeq0,\quad\operatorname{tr}C=1,\quad P(C)=p.
   108	\]
   109	
   110	**B. Two-mode orientation-preserving family, \(r=2\):**
   111	\[
   112	\Psi(X)=p U X U^\dagger+(1-p)\operatorname{tr}(X)I_2/2,
   113	\quad U\text{ unitary},\quad0\leq p\leq1.
   114	\]
   115	
   116	**C. Two-mode orientation-reversing family, \(r=2\):**
   117	\[
   118	\Psi(X)=p U X^{\mathsf T} U^\dagger+(1-p)\operatorname{tr}(X)I_2/2,
   119	\quad U\text{ unitary},\quad0\leq p\leq1/3.
   120	\]
   121	
   122	For \(r=1\), only A occurs. For \(r\geq3\), only A occurs. At \(p=0\) the families overlap. The transpose is in a fixed orthonormal basis of the whitened two-mode input space. These conditions preserve the **degree**, not necessarily the polarization ellipse/orientation.
   123	
   124	### Proof, including the higher-dimensional exclusion
   125	
   126	For a unit input vector \(f\), the normalized output is a density matrix. Write its Bloch vector as \(b(f)\in\mathbb R^3\). A general qubit-input trace-preserving Hermiticity-preserving map is affine on Bloch vectors:
   127	
   128	\[
   129	b=a+M x,\qquad |x|=1,
   130	\]
   131	
   132	with real \(a\) and real \(3\times3\) matrix \(M\). Constant output degree \(p\) gives
   133	
   134	\[
   135	|a|^2+2a^{\mathsf T}Mx+x^{\mathsf T}M^{\mathsf T}Mx=p^2\quad (|x|=1).
   136	\]
   137	
   138	Replacing \(x\) by \(-x\) yields \(M^{\mathsf T}a=0\). A real symmetric matrix whose quadratic form is constant on the unit sphere is a scalar multiple of identity (evaluate on coordinate vectors and their pairwise sums), so \(M^{\mathsf T}M=\lambda I_3\). If \(\lambda=0\), \(M=0\), \(|a|=p\), giving A. Otherwise \(M\) is invertible, \(a=0\), and \(M=p O\), where \(O\in O(3)\). Every rotation in \(SO(3)\) is induced by a unitary conjugation on qubit matrices; reflection \((x,y,z)\mapsto(x,-y,z)\) is transposition. Thus these two components of \(O(3)\) give B and C.
   139	
   140	Complete positivity supplies the stated ranges rather than being assumed from mere output positivity. With \(|\Omega\rangle=|00\rangle+|11\rangle\) and swap \(F\), the Choi matrices before unitary conjugation are
   141	
   142	\[
   143	\Gamma_B=(1-p)I_4/2+p|\Omega\rangle\langle\Omega|,
   144	\qquad
   145	\Gamma_C=(1-p)I_4/2+pF.
   146	\]
   147	
   148	Their eigenvalues are respectively \((1+3p)/2\) once and \((1-p)/2\) three times; and \((1+p)/2\) three times and \((1-3p)/2\) once. Positive semidefiniteness gives \(p\leq1\) and \(p\leq1/3\). A's Choi matrix is \(I_r\otimes C\), which is positive. Each displayed map has output Bloch length \(p\) for unit pure inputs, proving sufficiency in dimensions one and two.
   149	
   150	Now suppose \(r\geq3\), \(p>0\), and the map is not replacement. Every two-dimensional input compression is a trace-preserving completely positive qubit map with the same constant output degree, so by the preceding argument it is either constant or maps its input sphere onto the sphere of radius \(p\). Nonreplacement means two pure inputs have different outputs: otherwise the polarization identity would make the entire linear map replacement. Their span is a nonconstant two-dimensional compression. Choose orthonormal \(e_1,e_2\) in that span and output coordinates so their Bloch vectors are \(+p\hat z,-p\hat z\). Pick \(e_3\) orthogonal to that span. The two-dimensional classification applied to \(\operatorname{span}(e_1,e_3)\) implies that the output for \(e_3\) is either \(+p\hat z\) (constant compression) or \(-p\hat z\) (nonconstant compression maps orthogonal input states to antipodes). Exchanging \(e_1,e_2\) if needed, take it to be \(-p\hat z\).
   151	
   152	The compression to \(V=\operatorname{span}(e_2,e_3)\) must be constant: a nonconstant compression maps orthogonal inputs to different antipodes, whereas both these outputs are \(-p\hat z\). Hence every unit \(w\in V\) has output \(-p\hat z\). Define the Hermitian observable \(B_x=\Psi^*(\sigma_x)/p\). For every such \(w\), the compression to \(\operatorname{span}(e_1,w)\) is nonconstant and maps its north/south poles to \(\pm p\hat z\). Its Bloch action divided by \(p\) is orthogonal and fixes those poles; consequently its \(x\)-row has unit length in the input equatorial plane. In matrix form this says
   153	
   154	\[
   155	|\langle e_1,B_x w\rangle|=1\qquad\text{for every unit }w\in V.
   156	\]
   157	
   158	But \(w\mapsto\langle e_1,B_x w\rangle\) is a complex linear functional on the two-dimensional complex space \(V\), and has a nonzero kernel. Choosing a unit vector in that kernel is a contradiction. Thus the map is replacement. If \(p=0\), every pure input has output \(I_2/2\); linearity/polarization again gives replacement. This covers all cases. ∎
   159	
   160	**Source meaning.** Family A before whitening is \(\Phi(X)=\operatorname{tr}(TX)C\), equivalently \(\Gamma_{ab}=T_{ba}C\), so
   161	\(W(r_1,r_2)=g(r_1,r_2)C\).
   162	Families B/C are valid positive sources with nonzero cross-polarization correlations and generally varying local polarization direction. They need not be Schell-model sources. Any scalar Fresnel, scalar angular-spectrum, scalar ABCD, aperture or deterministic phase-screen probe is covered by sufficiency; necessity concerns **all** probes, not Fresnel propagation alone. Independent source modes make arbitrary amplitude vectors realizable by some scalar linear functional, but not necessarily by free-space point sampling alone.
   163	
   164	## C3. Universal random-kernel robustness forces factorization
   165	
   166	**Evidence: Certification. Novelty: elementary convexity consequence, not claimed new.**
   167	
   168	Under the finite-mode assumptions, replace a deterministic probe by an arbitrary incoherent mixture of scalar probes. Its modal response is any positive semidefinite matrix \(R=\mathbb E[ff^\dagger]\), and its local coherency matrix is \(\Phi(R)\). This presupposes a scalar medium statistically independent of the source. Fix \(p\). Then
   169	
   170	\[
   171	P[\Phi(R)]=p\text{ for every }R\succeq0\text{ with }\operatorname{tr}(TR)>0
   172	\]
   173	
   174	**if and only if** \(\Phi(X)=\operatorname{tr}(TX)C\), with \(P(C)=p\).
   175	
   176	**Proof.** Whiten as in C2. For any two pure inputs, let their output Bloch vectors be \(b_1,b_2\), each of length \(p\). The equal mixture has vector \((b_1+b_2)/2\), which must also have length \(p\). The identity
   177	\[
   178	|(b_1+b_2)/2|^2=p^2-|b_1-b_2|^2/4
   179	\]
   180	forces \(b_1=b_2\). For \(p=0\) this is automatic. All pure inputs therefore have the same output; their projectors span the Hermitian matrices, so linearity gives the replacement map. Its sufficiency is immediate. ∎
   181	
   182	For either exceptional two-mode family B/C,
   183	
   184	\[
   185	P[\Psi(R)]=p\sqrt{1-4\det R/(\operatorname{tr}R)^2}=p\,P(R).
   186	\]
   187	
   188	This follows from the same Bloch action \(b\mapsto pO b\). For \(p>0\), equality to \(p\) occurs exactly when \(R\) has rank one. The exact example \(R=(|0\rangle\langle0|+|1\rangle\langle1|)/2\) reduces degree \(p\) to zero although each realization has degree \(p\). It is a source/modal counterexample to deterministic-to-ensemble inference, **not** a claim that every atmospheric turbulence model realizes every \(R\).
   189	
   190	For any continuous positive kernel of factorized form \(W=gC\), scalar deterministic and scalar extended-Huygens–Fresnel ensemble propagation both give \(J=hC\), directly by pulling \(C\) outside the integral. This sufficiency needs neither finite rank nor a particular turbulence law. Necessity above remains a finite-mode, all-mixtures statement.
   191	
   192	## C4. Neither invariant implies the other
   193	
   194	**Evidence: Certification of analytic constructions; numerical propagation is Empirical evidence. Novelty: no priority claim.**
   195	
   196	### Polarization invariance does not imply spectral invariance
   197	
   198	Take the finite-energy two-dimensional coherent scalar source profile
   199	\(u_0(r)=\exp(-|r|^2/(2w^2))\), frequency-independent waist \(w\), source spectrum \(B(k)\), and constant polarization matrix \(C=\operatorname{diag}(3/4,1/4)\). Set
   200	\(W(r_1,r_2;k)=B(k)u_0(r_1)u_0(r_2)^* C\).
   201	C3 gives \(P=1/2\) at every illuminated point under any scalar channel. Fresnel propagation gives on-axis spectral intensity, relative to its initial value,
   202	
   203	\[
   204	I(0,z;k)/B(k)=\frac{1}{1+(z/(kw^2))^2}.
   205	\]
   206	
   207	It depends on \(k\); the off-axis Gaussian width is also frequency dependent. At \(z=w=1\), equal-weight spectral lines at \(k=1,2\) have on-axis weights \(1/2,4/5\), so normalized weights change from \((1/2,1/2)\) to \((5/13,8/13)\). These are rational identities, not floating-point evidence. The same failure holds for a continuous integrable spectrum positive near both frequencies. Already in the far zone, the angular spectrum is proportional to \(B(k)k^2e^{-w^2k^2|s|^2}\), which is not separable in \(s,k\).
   208	
   209	### Far-zone spectral invariance does not imply polarization invariance
   210	
   211	Take two statistically independent, orthogonally polarized coherent source modes in two dimensions,
   212	
   213	\[
   214	u_a(r;k)=\frac{k}{a^2}\exp[-k^2|r|^2/(2a^2)],\quad
   215	u_b(r;k)=\frac{k}{b^2}\exp[-k^2|r|^2/(2b^2)],\quad a=1,b=2.
   216	\]
   217	
   218	Their powers are finite; set \(W_{xx}=B(k)u_a(r_1;k)u_a(r_2;k)^*\), \(W_{yy}=B(k)u_b(r_1;k)u_b(r_2;k)^*\), \(W_{xy}=0\). Direct Fourier integration yields
   219	\(\widehat u_a(ks;k)=2\pi k^{-1}e^{-a^2|s|^2/2}\). After the common Fraunhofer factor,
   220	
   221	\[
   222	J(s;k)\propto B(k)\begin{pmatrix}e^{-|s|^2}&0\\0&e^{-4|s|^2}\end{pmatrix}.
   223	\]
   224	
   225	The normalized spectrum is independent of \(s\), but
   226	\[
   227	P(s;k)=\frac{|e^{-|s|^2}-e^{-4|s|^2}|}{e^{-|s|^2}+e^{-4|s|^2}}
   228	\]
   229	is zero at \(s=0\) and \(1/3\) at \(|s|^2=\log 2/3\). Thus it is not invariant across the far zone. This construction asserts **far-zone spectrum invariance**, not equality to every source-plane local spectrum. Both implication counterexamples apply to the same far-zone observation set.
   230	
   231	## C5. Joint invariance and the chromaticity boundary
   232	
   233	**Evidence: Certification. Novelty: elementary operator criterion, not claimed new.**
   234	
   235	Consider a family \(\Phi_\omega\) in the **same finite-dimensional input basis** and the same achromatic probe vector \(f\) at every frequency. Let \(T_\omega=\Phi_\omega^*(I_2)\). For positive spectral factor \(B(\omega)\), spectral invariance for every probe is equivalent to
   236	
   237	\[
   238	T_\omega=B(\omega)T
   239	\]
   240	
   241	for one positive matrix \(T\). **Proof:** the intensity is \(f^\dagger T_\omega f\). Subtract \(B(\omega)/B(\omega_0)\) times the equation at a reference frequency; vanishing of every quadratic form implies vanishing of the Hermitian matrix, by polarization. The converse is substitution. ∎
   242	
   243	Joint deterministic invariance is therefore precisely this trace condition plus one of C2's whitened families at each frequency. The spatially constant degree may be \(p(\omega)\); requiring a degree constant in frequency is the additional condition \(p(\omega)=p\).
   244	
   245	Joint invariance for every incoherent mixture of the same probes is precisely
   246	
   247	\[
   248	\Phi_\omega(X)=B(\omega)\operatorname{tr}(TX)C_\omega,
   249	\quad\operatorname{tr}C_\omega=1,
   250	\]
   251	
   252	with the prescribed degree \(P(C_\omega)\), by C3. This is an actual source condition, not an assumption that trace and determinant are independent for arbitrary positive kernels.
   253	
   254	In physical Fresnel/ABCD systems, \(f=f(x,\omega)\) is generally chromatic. Then a common \(T\) alone does **not** give spectral invariance: the additional scalar response \(f(x,\omega)^\dagger T f(x,\omega)\) must itself separate into spatial and spectral factors. C1 gives the appropriate far-zone condition. C4 is an explicit negative control against dropping this caveat.
   255	
   256	## C6. What is and is not settled
   257	
   258	The C2 all-probe finite-mode problem and C3 all-mixture problem are fully settled by the proofs above. This does not fully solve Zhao's open-ended 2009 problem. Remaining precise gaps are:
   259	
   260	1. Classify positive kernels invariant under **only** the actual point-evaluation Fresnel family, allowing general infinite spatial rank; the arbitrary-probe necessity does not transfer to that restricted family.
   261	2. Treat longitudinal electric-field components, transversality projections and non-scalar Jones kernels in full Maxwell propagation with a specified three-dimensional polarization observable.
   262	3. For any particular random environment, characterize its attainable response cone \(\{\mathbb E[f f^\dagger]\}\), rather than replacing it by all positive matrices without proof.
   263	4. Establish whether the all-rank constant-output-Bloch-length classification in C2 is already explicit in quantum-channel or statistical-optics literature. Correctness is not novelty.
   264	
   265	**Most valuable next action:** resolve the C2 prior-art comparison against constant-output-purity channels, then test whether the two-mode probe curve realized by unrestricted Fresnel distances is rich enough to force the same affine-sphere identity. More brute-force turbulence simulations cannot close either logical gap.
